1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,085
Lemon juice tastes sour.

2
00:00:02,085 --> 00:00:03,468
Soap feels slippery.

3
00:00:03,468 --> 00:00:06,526
Mix an acid and a base,
and you get a salt.

4
00:00:06,926 --> 00:00:09,387
For years, chemists asked one question.

5
00:00:09,387 --> 00:00:11,627
What exactly makes an acid an acid?

6
00:00:12,077 --> 00:00:14,379
Aarav washes his bicycle with soapy water.

7
00:00:14,379 --> 00:00:16,173
It feels slippery on his hands.

8
00:00:16,523 --> 00:00:18,916
At lunch, a squeeze of lemon tastes sour.

9
00:00:18,916 --> 00:00:20,145
Opposites, but why?

10
00:00:20,645 --> 00:00:24,802
By the end, you will know three ideas
of acids and bases, each bigger than

11
00:00:24,802 --> 00:00:25,702
the last.

12
00:00:26,155 --> 00:00:27,594
First, what we notice.

13
00:00:27,944 --> 00:00:30,362
Acids taste sour and turn blue litmus red.

14
00:00:30,762 --> 00:00:35,146
Bases taste bitter, feel soapy,
and turn red litmus blue.

15
00:00:35,546 --> 00:00:39,839
When they meet, they cancel each
other and make a salt and water.

16
00:00:39,839 --> 00:00:43,299
Hydrochloric acid and sodium
hydroxide make common salt.

17
00:00:43,899 --> 00:00:46,135
Idea one, from Svante Arrhenius.

18
00:00:46,135 --> 00:00:50,011
Let's drop hydrochloric acid
into water on our molecule bench.

19
00:00:50,361 --> 00:00:51,816
It splits into ions.

20
00:00:51,816 --> 00:00:54,090
An ion is an atom with a charge.

21
00:00:54,090 --> 00:00:56,853
Here, a hydrogen ion and a chloride ion.

22
00:00:57,253 --> 00:00:59,706
So an acid gives hydrogen ions in water.

23
00:01:00,106 --> 00:01:02,798
But a hydrogen ion is just a bare proton.

24
00:01:02,798 --> 00:01:04,856
It is too small to stay alone.

25
00:01:04,856 --> 00:01:09,302
It grabs a water molecule and becomes
a hydronium ion, H three O plus.

26
00:01:09,702 --> 00:01:15,625
A base, like sodium hydroxide, gives
hydroxide ions, OH minus, in water.

27
00:01:16,025 --> 00:01:17,138
The weak spot.

28
00:01:17,138 --> 00:01:18,813
It only works in water.

29
00:01:18,813 --> 00:01:21,827
And ammonia has no OH, yet it is a base.

30
00:01:22,427 --> 00:01:26,226
Idea two, from Bronsted and
Lowry, in nineteen twenty three.

31
00:01:26,226 --> 00:01:27,868
Think of passing a ball.

32
00:01:28,218 --> 00:01:30,310
An acid is a proton giver.

33
00:01:30,310 --> 00:01:32,347
A base is a proton taker.

34
00:01:32,747 --> 00:01:37,516
So an acid base reaction is just one
proton passing from one particle to

35
00:01:37,516 --> 00:01:38,416
another.

36
00:01:38,920 --> 00:01:41,600
Let's follow one proton, step by step.

37
00:01:41,950 --> 00:01:42,850
Step one.

38
00:01:42,705 --> 00:01:44,023
Ammonia meets water.

39
00:01:44,773 --> 00:01:45,673
Step two.

40
00:01:45,593 --> 00:01:47,803
Water gives a proton to ammonia.

41
00:01:47,803 --> 00:01:49,193
Water is the acid.

42
00:01:49,193 --> 00:01:50,683
Ammonia is the base.

43
00:01:51,433 --> 00:01:52,370
Step three.

44
00:01:52,370 --> 00:01:55,190
Ammonia becomes ammonium, NH four plus.

45
00:01:55,190 --> 00:01:57,694
Water becomes hydroxide, OH minus.

46
00:01:58,444 --> 00:01:59,344
Step four.

47
00:01:59,312 --> 00:02:00,538
Run it backward.

48
00:02:00,538 --> 00:02:04,137
Ammonium can give the proton
back, so it is an acid.

49
00:02:04,137 --> 00:02:06,968
Hydroxide can take it, so it is a base.

50
00:02:07,718 --> 00:02:09,629
These partners have a name.

51
00:02:09,629 --> 00:02:10,806
Conjugate pairs.

52
00:02:11,156 --> 00:02:14,003
A conjugate pair differs
by just one proton.

53
00:02:14,403 --> 00:02:16,717
Ammonia and ammonium are one pair.

54
00:02:16,717 --> 00:02:19,031
Water and hydroxide are the other.

55
00:02:19,431 --> 00:02:22,471
A strong acid gives
away its proton easily.

56
00:02:22,471 --> 00:02:25,775
So its partner, the
conjugate base, is weak.

57
00:02:26,175 --> 00:02:29,007
Water can act as an acid or as a base.

58
00:02:29,007 --> 00:02:30,645
We call it amphoteric.

59
00:02:31,245 --> 00:02:32,145
Compare.

60
00:02:31,989 --> 00:02:35,932
With ammonia, water gives
a proton, so it is an acid.

61
00:02:35,932 --> 00:02:40,410
With hydrochloric acid, water
takes a proton, so it is a base.

62
00:02:40,810 --> 00:02:42,054
Pause and think.

63
00:02:42,054 --> 00:02:46,920
When HC l gives its proton to water,
what is left behind as the conjugate

64
00:02:46,920 --> 00:02:47,820
base?

65
00:02:50,686 --> 00:02:51,586
Chloride.

66
00:02:51,456 --> 00:02:55,446
Take one proton away from
HC l, and you get C l minus.

67
00:02:56,096 --> 00:03:00,469
Idea three, from GN Lewis,
also in nineteen twenty three.

68
00:03:00,469 --> 00:03:03,043
Not every reaction passes a proton.

69
00:03:03,393 --> 00:03:06,058
Lewis looked at electron pairs instead.

70
00:03:06,058 --> 00:03:08,630
A Lewis base gives an electron pair.

71
00:03:08,630 --> 00:03:10,400
A Lewis acid takes one.

72
00:03:10,800 --> 00:03:13,040
Boron trifluoride is short of electrons.

73
00:03:13,040 --> 00:03:16,233
Ammonia has a lone pair, a
pair not used in any bond.

74
00:03:16,633 --> 00:03:20,554
Ammonia shares its pair with
boron, and a new bond forms.

75
00:03:20,554 --> 00:03:23,002
Boron trifluoride is the Lewis acid.

76
00:03:23,002 --> 00:03:24,881
Ammonia is the Lewis base.

77
00:03:25,281 --> 00:03:29,436
Electron short particles like aluminium
chloride and the magnesium ion

78
00:03:29,436 --> 00:03:30,668
are Lewis acids.

79
00:03:30,668 --> 00:03:33,691
Water, ammonia and
hydroxide are Lewis bases.

80
00:03:34,291 --> 00:03:35,191
Remember this.

81
00:03:35,174 --> 00:03:36,651
Bronsted is about protons.

82
00:03:36,651 --> 00:03:38,342
Lewis is about electron pairs.

83
00:03:39,042 --> 00:03:40,732
Now, one worked example.

84
00:03:41,032 --> 00:03:43,300
Find the conjugate base of three acids.

85
00:03:43,300 --> 00:03:46,408
HF, sulphuric acid, and
the hydrogen carbonate ion.

86
00:03:46,908 --> 00:03:47,808
The rule.

87
00:03:47,694 --> 00:03:51,000
Take away one proton, and
lower the charge by one.

88
00:03:51,750 --> 00:03:53,586
HF loses a proton.

89
00:03:53,586 --> 00:03:56,975
Its conjugate base is fluoride, F minus.

90
00:03:57,725 --> 00:04:01,644
Sulphuric acid, H two SO
four, becomes HSO four minus.

91
00:04:01,644 --> 00:04:06,140
Hydrogen carbonate, HCO three minus,
becomes carbonate, CO three two

92
00:04:06,140 --> 00:04:07,040
minus.

93
00:04:07,476 --> 00:04:10,016
Going the other way, add one proton.

94
00:04:10,016 --> 00:04:12,755
The conjugate acid of ammonia is ammonium.

95
00:04:13,405 --> 00:04:14,869
Now, the exam trap.

96
00:04:15,219 --> 00:04:18,883
Students take away the hydrogen,
but forget to change the charge.

97
00:04:19,283 --> 00:04:21,216
A proton carries a plus charge.

98
00:04:21,216 --> 00:04:23,702
Remove it, and the charge drops by one.

99
00:04:23,702 --> 00:04:26,574
HCO three minus becomes
CO three two minus.

100
00:04:27,224 --> 00:04:28,591
Where is this asked?

101
00:04:28,591 --> 00:04:32,657
Boards ask for conjugate acids and
bases, and to sort Lewis acids from

102
00:04:32,657 --> 00:04:33,557
bases.

103
00:04:33,217 --> 00:04:37,614
Entrance tests ask which particle is
a Lewis acid but not a Bronsted acid.

104
00:04:38,214 --> 00:04:39,335
Exam level now.

105
00:04:39,335 --> 00:04:43,012
Which of these is a Lewis
acid, but not a Bronsted acid?

106
00:04:43,012 --> 00:04:44,523
A, the ammonium ion.

107
00:04:44,523 --> 00:04:46,024
B, boron trifluoride.

108
00:04:46,024 --> 00:04:46,924
C, water.

109
00:04:46,898 --> 00:04:47,886
D, HC l.

110
00:04:47,886 --> 00:04:49,055
Pause and solve.

111
00:04:54,455 --> 00:04:55,635
The answer is B.

112
00:04:55,635 --> 00:04:56,797
Boron trifluoride.

113
00:04:57,297 --> 00:05:01,029
It has no hydrogen to give, so
it cannot be a Bronsted acid.

114
00:05:01,529 --> 00:05:06,003
But boron has room for an electron
pair, so it is a Lewis acid.

115
00:05:06,503 --> 00:05:09,364
Ammonium, water and HC
l can all give a proton.

116
00:05:09,364 --> 00:05:10,782
They are Bronsted acids.

117
00:05:11,382 --> 00:05:12,922
Here is your recap.

118
00:05:12,922 --> 00:05:14,505
Screenshot this card.

119
00:05:17,705 --> 00:05:19,891
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

120
00:05:20,291 --> 00:05:21,191
One.

121
00:05:20,640 --> 00:05:22,051
What does a Bronsted base do?

122
00:05:22,851 --> 00:05:23,751
Two.

123
00:05:23,359 --> 00:05:26,345
Write the conjugate base of H two SO four.

124
00:05:27,145 --> 00:05:28,045
Three.

125
00:05:27,611 --> 00:05:29,573
Why is boron trifluoride a Lewis acid?

126
00:05:30,473 --> 00:05:33,986
Next time: even pure water
splits a tiny bit into ions.

127
00:05:33,986 --> 00:05:35,575
How do we measure that?

128
00:05:35,575 --> 00:05:38,854
We find out in Ionization
of Water and the pH Scale.
